        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Pan-Islamism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1875 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a> (1839–1897), who sought Muslim unity against colonial occupation; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a> (1849–1905), his student, who favoured reform through religion and education; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a> (1865–1935), who argued for a restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a>; the classical scholars al-Mawardi, al-Ghazali and Fakhr al-Din al-Razi, who used the term ummah politically; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-qutb">Sayyid Qutb</a>, Abul Ala Maududi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ayatollah Khomeini</a> among the later Islamists; Necmettin Erbakan, a Turkish prime minister who championed a Pan-Islamic Union</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">al-Mawardi’s Al-Ahkam al-Sultaniyyah; al-Ghazali’s Fadiah al-Batinyah wa Fadail al-Mustazhariyah; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a>’s al-Khilafa aw al-Imama al-‘Uzma (“The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> or the Exalted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a>”), formulated in the 1920s, and his journal Al-Manar; Lothrop Stoddard’s The New World of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> (1921)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">the ummah, the Muslim community, as the focus of allegiance and mobilisation; Muslim unity under one Islamic state, often a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and the sunnah as guide; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhid">Tawhid</a>; religion rather than ethnicity or race as the basis of unity; opposition to nationalism; resistance to colonial rule</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Organisation of Islamic Conference, which grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> summit of 1969, is a permanent body of Muslim states; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a>, Jamaat-e-Islami and Hizb-ut-Tahrir are among the movements that carry the idea; the Developing 8 Countries, founded in 1996, still meet and carry out moderate collaboration projects</span></div>
