    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Pan-Islamism holds that Muslims should be united under one Islamic state, often a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, and puts the ummah, not ethnicity or race, at the centre of allegiance, which sets it apart from ideologies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism</a>.</li><li>Its modern leaders were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a>, who worked against European penetration of Muslim lands; Afghani sought change from above, Abduh from below through education, and Rida a restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>.</li><li>After the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in 1924 Rida’s ideas mobilised Muslims, and Arab nationalists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> repressed pan-Islamist movements; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> summit of 1969 led to the Organisation of Islamic Conference.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Lothrop Stoddard counted the Pan-Islamic press in 1921: in 1900 there were not more than 200 propagandist journals in the whole Islamic world, by 1906 there were 500, and in 1914 well over 1000.</p></aside>
    </section>

