    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">Late 19th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> promotes pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> to prevent secession movements of its Muslim peoples</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1902</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a> sets out his vision of Islamic unity in Al-Manar</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1914</span><span class="rf-text">The Pan-Islamic press has grown to well over 1000 journals, by Lothrop Stoddard’s count</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">The abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> leads pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a> to mobilise Muslim masses inspired by Rida’s ideas</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1969</span><span class="rf-text">The first summit of Muslim heads of state meets at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> and becomes the Organisation of Islamic Conference</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1979</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a> ousts Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-reza-pahlavi">Mohammad Reza Pahlavi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1989</span><span class="rf-text">The Afghan mujahideen, with major support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, force the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1996</span><span class="rf-text">Necmettin Erbakan founds the Developing 8 Countries</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio.jpg" alt="Usman dan Fodio" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Usman_Ibn_Fodio_Calligraphy_02.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anasskoko, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Islamism is an internationalist and anti-nationalist political movement that advocates the unity of Muslims under one Islamic state, often a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> or an international organisation with Islamic principles. It draws inspiration from early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, the era of Muhammad and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a>. Classical scholars such as al-Mawardi, al-Ghazali and Fakhr al-Din al-Razi had already used the term ummah in a political sense. Many scholars assert its doctrines can be seen as early as Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the Ottoman, Safavid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empires</a>.</p><p>In the 18th century several movements for puritanical Islamic renewal arose. The revivalist movements of Shah Wali Allah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> (1702–1763), Muhammad Ibn ‘Abd al-Wahhab (1703–1792) and Uthman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">dan Fodio</a> (1755–1816) are widely regarded as precursors of modern pan-Islamist thought.They called for scripture-based piety but were not politically concerned with the international situation of the Muslim world and did not call for the revival of an international Islamic entity, and their impact stayed local to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p><p>Reformers at the turn of the 19th century oscillated between admiration for Western progress and rejection of it; Rifa‘a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rifaa-al-tahtawi">al-Tahtawi</a> (d. 1872) exemplified the former, ‘Abd al-Rahman al-Jabarti (d. 1825) the latter. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, after Ottomanism, which aimed at the unity of all Ottoman citizens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> promoted pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a> in the last quarter of the 19th century to prevent secession movements among the empire’s Muslim peoples.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani.jpg" alt="Jamal al-Din al-Afghani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_afghani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Islamism differs from nationalist ideologies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism</a> by focusing on religion and not on ethnicity or race. It sees the ummah as the focus of allegiance and mobilisation, together with the belief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhid">Tawhid</a>, under the guidance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and the sunnah. The Arabic term ummah occurs in the Quran and Islamic tradition and has historically denoted the Muslims as a whole, regardless of race or ethnicity.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a> feared that nationalism would divide the Muslim world and held that Muslim unity mattered more than ethnic identity. Although sometimes called liberal, he did not advocate constitutional government; his biographer found nothing in his Paris-based newspaper that favoured political democracy or parliamentarianism, and he envisaged the overthrow of rulers who were lax or subservient to foreigners and their replacement by strong, patriotic men.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a> believed that the unification of the Islamic community would be possible only through the restoration of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> that implements the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a>. In 1902 he wrote in Al-Manar that Islamic unity meant that the scholars and notables should compile a book of ordinances based on the fundamentals of the Divine Law, which a Supreme <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> would order the rulers of Muslims to apply. In the 1920s he set out a doctrine of the Islamic state in al-Khilafa aw al-Imama al-‘Uzma, calling on Muslims to build a political system based on faith rather than nationalism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh.jpg" alt="Muhammad Abduh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Abduh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-rida">Rashid Rida</a> were the major leaders of the modern movement, active in anti-colonial efforts against European penetration of Muslim lands, and they held Islamic unity to be the strongest force for mobilising Muslims against imperial domination. Rida’s Al-Manar promoted anti-British revolt and Islamic revivalism based on the tenets of Salafiyya. Lothrop Stoddard, in 1921, took the growth of the Pan-Islamic press as a measure of the movement’s rising importance, counting not more than 200 propagandist journals in 1900, 500 by 1906 and well over 1000 in 1914.</p><p>After the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in 1924, pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a> mobilised Muslim masses of both traditionalist and reform movements, inspired by Rida. During the 1920s Rida and his Salafi disciples established the Young Men’s Muslim Association, an Islamist youth organisation. Pan-Islamic networks led by Rida and his associates played a central role in the later development of Islamist movements.</p><p>In the 1950s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>’s government championed Muslim cooperation. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>, Islamism and pan-Islamism gained popularity relative to nationalism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism</a>, and in 1969 the first summit of Muslim heads of state met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>. After 1979 the branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a>, Jamaat-e-Islami in Pakistan and, since the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, Hizb-ut-Tahrir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> became prominent pan-Islamist forces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii.jpg" alt="Abdul Hamid II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Gazi_Abdül_Hamid_II_-_السلطان_الغازي_عبد_الحميد_الثاني.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pan-Islamism was used as state policy before it became a movement against states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> promoted it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the last quarter of the 19th century to hold the empire’s Muslim peoples together. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>’s conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> bolstered pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a> across the Islamic world. At the height of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, in the 1960s and 1970s, Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and allied countries in the Muslim world led the pan-Islamist struggle against the spread of communist ideology and the rising Soviet influence in the world.</p><p>During the second half of the 20th century pan-Islamists competed against left-wing nationalist ideologies in the Arab world, such as Nasserism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baathism">Ba’athism</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> secular pan-Arab parties, Baath and Nasserist, had offshoots in almost every Arab country and took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Islamists suffered severe repression: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-qutb">Sayyid Qutb</a> was imprisoned, tortured and later executed. Egyptian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>, who considered the idea of Muslim unity a threat to Arab nationalism, took control of the mosques and appointed his own religious officials, and the religious establishment he created denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a> as inauthentic representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p>Prime Minister Necmettin Erbakan founded the Developing 8 Countries in 1996, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, Egypt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, Malaysia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, but his government was taken down by the so-called Post-Modern Coup of 28 February 1997.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser.jpg" alt="Gamal Abdel Nasser" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevic,_Gamal_Abdel_Naser_u_Beogradu,_1962_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevan Kragujević, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Within the movement the founders disagreed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Abduh</a> criticised Afghani and the pan-Islamist intellectuals for their political activities and judged education a more effective instrument of reform, while Afghani had bitter arguments with Abduh. The doctrines also divided Muslims on who should lead: Rida called for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and after 1979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Islamists such as Maududi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a> embraced a new caliphate as at least a long-term project, whereas Khomeini also embraced a united Islamic supra-state but saw it led by a Shia religious scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">fiqh</a>, a faqih.</p><p>Nationalists and secularists opposed the idea. Afghani feared that nationalism would divide the Muslim world, and Islamists in turn denounced nationalism as un-Islamic. In the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Arab nationalism overshadowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> saw Muslim unity as a threat to Arab nationalism. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> Rida and his disciples became the biggest adversaries of secularists and nationalists and vehemently attacked all forms of democratic ideas.</p><p>Governments were wary of one another’s pan-Islamic claims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>’s efforts in the 1950s were complicated by its part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad-pact">Baghdad Pact</a> and its pro-Western diplomacy, and many Muslim countries suspected that Pakistan aspired to leadership of the Muslim world in order to help Western powers in their relations with other Muslim states. Later relations were much better.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early pan-Islamist movement in the post-colonial world was strongly associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a>. Rida’s socio-political views marked the convergence of the doctrines of the reformist, Salafist and pan-Islamist movements, and his networks played a central role in the later development of Islamist movements. After the defeat of the Arab armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> dropped his opposition to a pan-Islamic platform, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> summit of 1969 was eventually transformed into a permanent body, the Organisation of Islamic Conference.</p><p>The events of 1979 and 1989 galvanised Islamists the world over and heightened their popularity with the Muslim public. Branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a> challenged secular nationalist or monarchical Muslim governments throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, in particular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> Jamaat-e-Islami enjoyed popular support, especially since the formation of the MMA, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> the FIS was expected to win the elections cancelled in 1992. Since the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> Hizb-ut-Tahrir has emerged as a pan-Islamist force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><p>Erbakan, a recent advocate, championed the Pan-Islamic Union, an Islamic Union (İslam Birliği), and his vision was a gradual unity of Muslim nations through economic and technological cooperation, similar to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, with a single monetary unit and joint aerospace and defence projects. The Developing 8 Countries still meet at presidential and cabinet levels and moderate collaboration projects continue, though its momentum was lost after the coup of 1997.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Pan-Islamism advocates the unity of Muslims under one Islamic state, often a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, with the ummah rather than ethnicity as the focus of allegiance. It took modern form in the late 19th century with Afghani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Abduh</a> and Rida, it later competed with Arab nationalism and led to the Organisation of Islamic Conference.</em></p>

